    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The full suite passes: **66 tests run, 0 failures, 0 errors, BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `capacityWarning` feature so that a newly created owner is flagged when its city already holds between 40 and 49 owners (approaching the hard limit of 50). The hard rejection at 50 (409 conflict) is unchanged.\n\nChanges made:\n\n1. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 in `addOwner`, computed the city's existing owner count once, kept the existing `>= 50` rejection, and set a `capacityWarning` flag true when the count is `>= 40` (the new `CITY_CAPACITY_WARNING_THRESHOLD` constant). The flag is persisted on the owner alongside `bulkSignupWarning`.\n\n2. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added the `capacityWarning` field (mapped to a `capacity_warning` column) with getter/setter.\n\n3. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the read-only `capacityWarning` boolean to the owner DTO schema (which generates `OwnerDto`; MapStruct auto-maps it by name).\n\n4. **Schema files** (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `mysql`, `postgres`) \u2014 added the `capacity_warning BOOLEAN` column so the flag round-trips through persistence (the tests use the hsqldb + spring-data-jpa profile, which reads the fresh owner back via GET).\n\nThe target test `Cp54Tests` (fills a city to 40, then asserts the 41st owner has `capacityWarning: true`) passes, as does `Cp18Tests` (capacity rejection at 50) and the full 66-test suite.",
